      "title": "Dams & Reservoirs in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s reservoirs through water supply, sedimentation, salinity, and the distinction between storage capacity and available water."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "infrastructure-and-water",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Storage is an operating system",
        "content": "A reservoir is more than a lake behind a wall. Its usefulness depends on how much water arrives, how much remains available for release, and whether that water has the quality required by its users. Research on Iran’s reservoirs examines water supply, power generation, flood control, and sediment management together because these functions share the same physical storage. Dez Reservoir, on the Dez River in southwestern Iran, provides a particularly clear example: sediment accumulation affects several services simultaneously. [s1]\n\nFor readers interpreting reports about water shortages, design capacity and actual stored water answer different questions. Capacity describes the space a reservoir was designed to provide; stored volume describes water present at a particular time. A national study of Iran’s aquifers reports substantial unused reservoir capacity over its historical assessment period and links limited surface-water availability with greater reliance on groundwater. That finding concerns a documented period, rather than the present filling level of every dam. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dez-sediment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dez: the reservoir also stores sediment",
        "content": "Rivers transport sediment as well as water. At Dez, researchers describe large sediment inputs during heavily sediment-laden floods and consequences for electricity generation, flood control, and water supply. Their assessment compared several responses: reducing erosion upstream, passing sediment through the reservoir, releasing sediment-rich bottom flows, and routing sediment around storage. [s1]\n\nThese methods are constrained by existing engineering. The study notes that Dez lacks the sluice gates needed for some approaches. It also evaluates interventions that could reduce deposition; these are assessed strategies, not evidence that every proposed intervention was subsequently built or achieved its modeled benefit. The distinction matters when reading claims that an aging reservoir’s capacity can simply be restored. A technically plausible remedy still needs suitable outlets, operating water, funding, and demonstrated results. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gotvand-water-quality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Upper Gotvand: water quality changes the calculation",
        "content": "Upper Gotvand, on the Karun River, illustrates a different constraint. A published investigation identifies severe reservoir salinization associated with a setting containing exposed evaporites: rocks, including salt-bearing deposits, formed through evaporation. Its inspected abstract establishes the salinization problem; it does not support a claim about the reservoir’s condition at the editorial cutoff. [s3]\n\nThe wider lesson is that a cubic metre of stored water is not automatically a cubic metre suitable for every purpose. Reservoir assessment must consider surrounding geology and dissolved salts alongside volume. A full appraisal of Upper Gotvand’s downstream effects would additionally require dated release records and downstream water-quality measurements. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "basin-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the basin, not just the dam",
        "content": "Iran’s water pressures extend beyond individual structures. Research using satellite gravimetry, precipitation information, and groundwater observations found declining total water storage during its historical study period despite an overall increase in precipitation relative to its comparison period. This supports examining extraction and management alongside weather; it does not attribute every local shortage to one dam. [s4]\n\nA useful account of any reservoir therefore asks about its inflows, usable storage, sediment, water quality, releases, and connections with groundwater. Construction alone cannot establish dependable supply. The practical question is what service the reservoir can sustain within the limits of its basin. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
